[38;5;231m⏺[39m That gives me a coherent shape. Now the question that defines both sub-projects at once, since what triggers a
  notification is exactly what fields a task needs.

  Since you made every change yourself in a single-user app, "a task changed" isn't worth a notification. What's left is
  time — and there are two different ways to model that, which is the part with consequences:

  - [1mDerived from the due date[0m — the system computes reminders (due soon, overdue) from [38;5;153mdueDate[39m plus a global lead-time
    setting. One field on the task, one preference. Simple, and every task with a date gets covered automatically.
  - [1mExplicit per-task reminders[0m — you set "remind me Thursday 3pm" independently of when it's due. More expressive, but
    it's a second scheduled-time concept on the task, and the scheduling logic has to handle both.

  Derived is the cheaper model and covers most of the value; per-task reminders can be added on top later without
  changing what's already there. Pick whichever of these you'd actually want — you can choose more than one.
